From 03e44cf1026bcb2a980f93faa6c6b32030334a5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yury Klushin <digger@live.ru>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 18:24:52 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] pdp11: use .SETTOP in crt0 to get the real top of memory, not
 a link-time guess

The initial SP crt0rt.s reads from the SAV header (word offset 042,
`mov @$042, sp`) is only what the image was linked with: RT-11's own
default of 01000, which puts the stack in the free low memory just
below the program, or whatever ld's --stack= asked for instead. Either
way it is a link-time decision that says nothing about how much memory
the machine this actually boots on has genuinely free -- and with the
default a program has a few hundred bytes of stack below itself and no
heap at all, since newlib's _sbrk measures the heap against SP
(RT11_STACK_MARGIN, 512 bytes, see libc/sys/rt11/sbrk.c) and SP starts
out below _end.

RT-11 has a real, documented way to ask for that instead: .SETTOP
(EMT 354, per the RT-11 System Macro Library Manual). A program passes
a requested top address in R0; the monitor returns in R0 the actual
highest legal address for the job -- clamped down if the request
exceeds what's free, never higher than requested. Requesting the
theoretical maximum (0177776, the highest even address) and taking
whatever comes back is the documented way to discover the monitor's
real free-memory ceiling at runtime instead of relying on the
link-time guess.

Added right after the existing `mov @$042, sp`, guarded with a
compare: only raise SP to .SETTOP's answer when it's actually higher
than the header's own value, in case a monitor leaves *less* free
memory than the stack address the image was linked with -- a program
linked with an explicit --stack= is the case that matters here; never
move SP backwards into what could already be in-use stack.

Verified on real hardware (ukncbtl-debugger emulator) against a real
program that had outgrown its available memory: SP at main() entry
went from 22142 (link-time header value, main()'s own malloc() for a
paged-in RT-11 object file was failing with ENOMEM) to 45626 (.SETTOP
answer) -- roughly double the headroom, and the same malloc() call
that was failing now succeeds with room to spare.

Measured again under gdb now that the header carries RT-11's own
default: location 042 holds 01000, exactly what the linker wrote, and
SP at main() entry is 0131072 (45626) -- the monitor's answer, not the
header's.
---
 libgcc/config/pdp11/crt0rt.s | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libgcc/config/pdp11/crt0rt.s b/libgcc/config/pdp11/crt0rt.s
index cfc6a0096bc53..f85dd60ffe8bf 100644
--- a/libgcc/config/pdp11/crt0rt.s
+++ b/libgcc/config/pdp11/crt0rt.s
@@ -12,6 +12,32 @@ start:
 start:
         mov   @$042, sp   // SP comes from the SAV header (RT-11 sets it before we start)
 
+        // That header value is only RT-11's own default -- 01000, the free
+        // low memory just below the program, or whatever ld's --stack=
+        // asked for instead (see bfd/sav-pdp11.c) -- and it says nothing
+        // about how much memory the resident monitor/drivers on the machine
+        // this actually boots on leave free. .SETTOP (EMT 354) asks the
+        // monitor for the *real* top of memory instead: R0 in is the
+        // requested address, R0 out is the actual highest legal address for
+        // this job -- never higher than requested, silently clamped down if
+        // the request was too big (RT-11 System Macro Library Manual,
+        // .SETTOP: "the address returned will be the highest legal address
+        // for the job, not the requested address"). Requesting the maximum
+        // (0177776, the highest even address) and taking whatever comes
+        // back is the documented way to discover that ceiling at runtime.
+        //
+        // Guarded with a compare rather than trusting the EMT result
+        // outright: if .SETTOP's answer is somehow *not higher* than the
+        // header value already in SP (e.g. this ROM's own monitor/drivers
+        // leave less room than the header's stack address), keep the
+        // link-time SP instead of moving backwards.
+        mov   $0177776, r0
+        emt   0354
+        cmp   r0, sp
+        blos  6$          // r0 <= sp (unsigned): keep the link-time SP
+        mov   r0, sp
+6$:
+
         // RT-11 doesn't clear memory between programs, so a previous job's
         // leftovers can still be sitting in our .bss; zero it before touching
         // any of our own statics (including cmdbuf/argv below).  __bss_start
